Fillets: the end of a rounding whose face runs on tangentially past the
vertex, cut by several faces (mitcad#133).

The case: the top edge of a window through a slab rounded, where at the
window's end the slab's face turns tangentially into the end wall (an arc
of a cylinder, then a flat across the slab). At the vertex the edges are in
the OnSame state: the contact line on the slab's face does not end on any
edge, it runs on past the vertex, while the contact on the window's ceiling
ends on the flat. ChFi3d_Builder::PerformIntersectionAtEnd handles an
OnSame end (it prolongs the face's edge at the vertex to the contact and
updates the contact with IntersUpdateOnSame), but gave up as soon as a
contact point was off an edge (PerformMoreThreeCorner, which failed). Now:

- A three-edge vertex in the OnSame state whose contact on one side is
  off any edge takes the edge of that side's face at the vertex as the
  first (or last) edge when it is the one in that state, and the end is
  cut by the faces around (the arc, the flat), as for any intersection at
  end. Both sides are handled: the first contact off its edge with the
  first face's edge in the OnSame state, or the second contact off its
  edge with the second face's edge in it.
- When the updated contact point lies beyond the vertex on that edge's
  prolongation, the piece of its curve from the vertex to the point bounds
  the side's face and the face across the edge, each in the edge's own
  orientation in that face, instead of a point beyond the edge's end; and
  the spine's edge is cut at the vertex, as in PerformOneCorner's OnSame
  case (ProlongOnSameEdge).
- On a periodic face at the end, the ends of the intersection (from
  p-curves and projections in any period) are taken into the face's
  domain where a period brings them there.
